textosLlegan("theory", {"fil-t4":{"html":"<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>How to study this topic: here what matters is not <em>what</em> is said, but <em>how</em> one reasons. Learn to separate two things that are constantly confused: <strong>validity</strong> (the form of the reasoning is correct) and <strong>truth</strong> (the premises are true). The table of validity and truth and the list of fallacies are the best guide for revision.</p> </blockquote>\n<h2>1. What is logic?</h2>\n<p><strong>Logic</strong> is the branch of philosophy devoted to examining the <strong>validity of arguments</strong>. It is not interested in whether what is stated is true, but in whether conclusions <strong>derive</strong> adequately from the premises on which they rest. For this reason it is often said that it studies the <strong>structure</strong> of thought, not its <strong>matter</strong>. It was <strong>Aristotle</strong> who constituted it as an autonomous discipline, by formulating the theory of the <strong>syllogism</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Logic studies the form of reasoning; but before reasoning we already think in a <strong>language</strong>, and one may ask whether the language we speak shapes what we can think. Two North American linguists, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/sapir\">Edward Sapir</a></strong> (1884-1939) and his student <strong><a href=\"#biografias/whorf\">Benjamin Lee Whorf</a></strong> (1897-1941), formulated the <strong>hypothesis of linguistic relativity</strong>: each language, with its words and its grammar, carves up the world in its own way and guides the thinking of those who speak it. In its strong version (language <em>determines</em> thought) it is today considered exaggerated; in its weak version (language <em>influences</em> what we pay attention to) it remains an <strong>open debate</strong>.</p>\n<p>From the Basque language, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/txillardegi\">Txillardegi</a></strong> (1929-2012), a writer and linguist, thought something similar: for him, a language is not just an instrument of communication, but a way of its own of seeing and ordering the world, so that to lose a language is also to lose a way of looking.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: do you think the same in the different languages you know, or does each make you notice different things? If a language disappears, is only a code lost, or also a way of thinking? You will meet Sapir, Whorf and Txillardegi in Year 2, in the philosophy of language.</p>\n<h2>2. Propositions and arguments</h2>\n<p>A <strong>proposition</strong> is a statement of which it makes sense to say that it is <strong>true or false</strong> (‘it is raining’, ‘7 is prime’). An <strong>argument</strong> is a set of propositions in which some, the <strong>premises</strong>, are offered as support for another, the <strong>conclusion</strong>. To argue is to give <strong>reasons</strong>: it is the opposite of imposing or repeating.</p>\n<p>A proposition must not be confused with the <strong>sentence</strong> that expresses it: ‘Llueve’, ‘It’s raining’ and ‘Euria ari du’ are three different sentences, in three languages, but they say the same thing: the same proposition. Aristotle had already set this kind of speech—the kind that affirms or denies something of something—apart from other kinds that are neither true nor false, such as a plea or a command (<em>On Interpretation</em>, 4-5). ‘Shut the door!’ is neither true nor false.</p>\n<p>Traditional logic used the word <strong>judgement</strong> for the act of the mind that affirms or denies, and the word proposition for the result of that act, what is left thought. Today we put it like this: a judgement is made by someone at a particular moment; the proposition stays the same whoever thinks it.</p>\n<p>The simplest form of proposition, according to classical logic, is <strong>S is P</strong>: a subject (S), a predicate (P) and the copula ‘is’ that joins them. ‘Ane runs’ is rewritten as ‘Ane is someone who runs’.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Deduction and induction</h2>\n<p>There are two main ways of reasoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deduction</strong>: the conclusion follows from the premises with <strong>necessity</strong>. If the premises are true, the conclusion cannot be false. It is the reasoning of mathematics and formal logic.</li>\n<li><strong>Induction</strong>: from particular cases one arrives at a <strong>general</strong> conclusion that is only <strong>probable</strong> (however many white swans you see, it is not certain that all of them are). It is the typical reasoning of the empirical sciences.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>4. Validity and truth</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-16\">This is the key distinction of the topic. <strong>Validity</strong> is a property of the <strong>form</strong>: an argument is valid when the conclusion follows correctly from the premises. <strong>Truth</strong> is a property of the <strong>content</strong>: the premises do or do not describe how things are. <strong>They are independent</strong>: there can be valid arguments with false premises, and invalid arguments with true premises. An argument is <strong>sound</strong> when it is <strong>valid and in addition</strong> has true premises.</p>\n<p>Strictly speaking, talk of validity only makes sense in <strong>deduction</strong>. Something else is asked of an induction: to be <strong>strong or weak</strong>, according to how much probability it lends its conclusion. Some add a third mode, <strong>abduction</strong> (Peirce) or <strong>inference to the best explanation</strong>: we start from a fact and settle on the hypothesis that best accounts for it. You get home, the kitchen floor is flooded and the dog is hiding: you conclude that it has knocked over its bowl. But we only compare the hypotheses we have in front of us; if it has not occurred to you that the dishwasher is leaking, you will end up with the best of a bad bunch (the objection is Bas van Fraassen’s).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Valid</th><th>Invalid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>True premises</td><td><strong>Sound</strong> (conclusion guaranteed)</td><td>Conclusion not guaranteed</td></tr><tr><td>False premises</td><td>Correct in form, but proves nothing</td><td>Doubly flawed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2>5. Formal and symbolic logic</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-20\">To avoid the ambiguities of everyday language, modern logic uses a <strong>formal language</strong> with <strong>symbols</strong>. <strong>Gottlob Frege</strong> created at the end of the 19th century <strong>first-order logic</strong> (or predicate logic), the basis of present-day logic. <strong>Russell and Whitehead</strong>, in the <em>Principia Mathematica</em>, tried to derive all of arithmetic from logic. This symbolic logic is also the foundation of computer science and artificial intelligence.</p>\n<p><strong>Complete and incomplete induction.</strong> Not all induction is equally risky. In <strong>complete induction</strong> every case of a class is examined, leaving none out. The tutor checks, pupil by pupil, that each of the twenty-five in her group has an assigned locker, and concludes that all of them have one. The conclusion is necessary, because it only summarises what has already been checked. The problem is that it is almost never feasible: one cannot examine all swans or all future mornings.</p><p>The usual case is <strong>incomplete induction</strong>, which generalises from a sample. You taste three oranges from a box, all three are sweet, and you conclude that all of them are. The conclusion goes beyond the data, and therefore it is only probable: the fourth may be sour even though the premises are true.</p><p>Bertrand Russell illustrated it in <em>The Problems of Philosophy</em> (1912, ch. VI, ‘On Induction’) with a chicken. The farmer feeds it every day, until one of those days he wrings its neck. The popular version replaces the chicken with a turkey that expects its food every morning, including on Christmas Eve. The premises (‘each day I have been fed’) were true, and the conclusion (‘tomorrow too’), false.</p><p>The root of the problem had been raised by David Hume in <em>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em> (1748, sections IV and V). No reasoning demonstrates that the future will resemble the past, because the contrary can be thought without contradiction. According to Hume, if we expect the sun to rise tomorrow it is not by proof, but by <strong>habit</strong>: repetition has taught us to expect the same.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what is the difference between a large sample and a representative sample? Do a thousand surveys carried out at the exit of a concert say much about what a city thinks? And why are you so sure that the sun will rise tomorrow?</p>\n\n<h2>6. Categorical propositions: A, E, I, O</h2>\n<p>Aristotle studied one kind of proposition above all. When S and P are groups of things, the proposition can speak about <strong>all</strong> of them or about <strong>some</strong> (its <strong>quantity</strong>: universal or particular), and it can <strong>affirm</strong> or <strong>deny</strong> (its <strong>quality</strong>: affirmative or negative). Combining the two gives four types, which since the Middle Ages have been named with vowels: A and I are the first two vowels of <em>affirmo</em> (‘I affirm’, in Latin), and E and O those of <em>nego</em> (‘I deny’).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Form</th><th>Example</th><th>In modern logic</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>A</strong> · universal affirmative</td><td>All S are P</td><td>All mammals are vertebrates.</td><td>∀x (Sx → Px)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>E</strong> · universal negative</td><td>No S are P</td><td>No fish are mammals.</td><td>¬∃x (Sx ∧ Px)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>I</strong> · particular affirmative</td><td>Some S are P</td><td>Some athletes are left-handed.</td><td>∃x (Sx ∧ Px)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>O</strong> · particular negative</td><td>Some S are not P</td><td>Some athletes are not left-handed.</td><td>∃x (Sx ∧ ¬Px)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The last column is the notation of predicate logic, which is read like this: ∀x, ‘for every x’; ∃x, ‘there is at least one x’; and Sx, ‘x is S’. ‘All S are P’ becomes ‘for anything at all, if it is S, then it is P’. Notice a detail that will matter later: A <strong>does not say that any S exist</strong>; it only says that if something is S, it is also P. I and O, on the other hand, begin with ‘there is’.</p>\n\n<h2>7. The square of opposition</h2>\n<p>The four propositions with the same subject and the same predicate are not independent: if we know whether one of them is true or false, we sometimes know something about the others. Tradition summed this up in a square, with A and E at the top, I and O at the bottom, and the relations along the sides and the diagonals:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Relation</th><th>Between</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Contradictories</strong> (diagonals)</td><td>A and O · E and I</td><td>They always have opposite values: if one is true, the other is false, and vice versa. To deny ‘all athletes are left-handed’, a single right-handed athlete is enough.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Contraries</strong> (top)</td><td>A and E</td><td>They cannot both be true, but they can both be false: ‘all athletes are left-handed’ and ‘no athletes are left-handed’ are both false.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Subcontraries</strong> (bottom)</td><td>I and O</td><td>They cannot both be false, but they can both be true: some athletes are left-handed and some athletes are not.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Subalterns</strong> (sides)</td><td>A → I · E → O</td><td>If the universal is true, so is the particular: if all mammals are vertebrates, some mammals are vertebrates.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘Some students have passed’ does not imply ‘some students have not passed’. In conversation we hint at it (‘some’, so not all), but logically I is compatible with A: if everyone has passed, it is also true that some have passed. Subcontraries can both be true; they do not have to be.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: ‘All unicorns have one horn’. Is it true, false or neither, if there are no unicorns at all? Keep your answer: we will come back to it in section 11.</p>\n\n<h2>8. From Euler to Venn</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-31\">The idea of drawing classes as closed figures became famous thanks to the mathematician <strong>Leonhard Euler</strong> (1707-1783). In 1761 he explained logic to a German princess in a series of letters (numbers 102 to 108), published in 1768. In them he uses circles: one circle inside another for ‘all’, separate circles for ‘none’ and overlapping circles for ‘some’. These are the Euler diagrams you already know from the Logic Corner.</p>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@128,112,40|P@150,100,88,150,36\"><figcaption><strong>A</strong> · All S are P: S inside P.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@80,100,62|P@220,100,62\"><figcaption><strong>E</strong> · No S are P: separate circles.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@115,100,72,88,64|P@185,100,72,212,64;x=150,100\"><figcaption><strong>I</strong> · Some S are P: overlapping circles, with someone in the shared area.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@115,100,72,88,64|P@185,100,72,212,64;x=82,104\"><figcaption><strong>O</strong> · Some S are not P: someone in S outside P.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>Euler was not the first. <strong>Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</strong> (1646-1716) had drawn the same circles around 1686, in a draft entitled <em>On the Testing of Logical Form by Drawing Lines</em>, and he himself said that he had seen them as a young man in a book by Johann Christoph Sturm (1661). But the draft remained unpublished until 1903, so Euler could not have known it. In that same paper Leibniz tried another way of drawing: each class is a <strong>segment</strong>. If the S segment falls inside the P segment, all S are P; if they do not touch, no S are P. The dotted stretches are the parts about which we do not know whether they contain anything or not.</p>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-lineas=\"S:120-230|P:50-330\"><figcaption><strong>A</strong> · All S are P: the S segment lies inside the P segment.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-lineas=\"S:50-160|P:220-330\"><figcaption><strong>E</strong> · No S are P: the two segments do not touch.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-lineas=\"S:50-230:50-160|P:160-330:230-330\"><figcaption><strong>I</strong> · Some S are P: they share a stretch; the dotted part may or may not exist.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-lineas=\"S:50-230:160-230|P:160-330\"><figcaption><strong>O</strong> · Some S are not P: a stretch of S lies outside P.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>You can compare the two methods, circles and lines, with more syllogisms in <a href=\"#leibniz\">Leibniz’s Workshop</a>.</p>\n<p>The problem with Euler diagrams is that you have to know in advance how the classes are arranged. In 1816-1817 the French mathematician Joseph Gergonne showed that between two classes there are only <strong>five possible situations</strong>: they are the same class, the first is inside the second, the second is inside the first, they overlap, or they are separate. ‘Some S are P’ is true in four of them, and a single Euler drawing cannot show them all at once:</p>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@150,100,72,128,52|P@150,100,72,172,52\"><figcaption>S and P are the same class.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@128,112,40|P@150,100,88,150,36\"><figcaption>S inside P.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@150,100,88,150,36|P@128,112,40\"><figcaption>P inside S.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@115,100,72,88,64|P@185,100,72,212,64\"><figcaption>S and P overlap. Only the fifth situation, separate circles, makes the proposition false.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>In 1880 the British logician <strong>John Venn</strong> (1834-1923) proposed another solution in an article in the journal <em>Philosophical Magazine</em>: always draw the circles overlapping, so that <strong>all the possible regions</strong> appear, and then mark what we know about each one. Venn showed the empty regions by shading them. Later, the American philosopher Charles S. Peirce added a mark to say that a region <strong>contains at least one thing</strong>: the ✕ we use here.</p>\n\n<h2>9. The four propositions in Venn diagrams</h2>\n<p>Venn diagrams that represent what a proposition <strong>asserts</strong> use three marks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grey</strong>: that region is empty. There is nothing in it.</li>\n<li><strong>✕</strong>: there is at least one thing in that region.</li>\n<li><strong>White</strong>: we know nothing. There may or may not be something there.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P;vac=10\"><figcaption><strong>A</strong> · All S are P: there is no S outside P.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P;vac=11\"><figcaption><strong>E</strong> · No S are P: the shared area is empty.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P;x=11\"><figcaption><strong>I</strong> · Some S are P: there is at least one in the shared area.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P;x=10\"><figcaption><strong>O</strong> · Some S are not P: there is at least one S outside P.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>Now the square of opposition can be seen in a new way. A empties the region ‘S outside P’, and O puts an ✕ right there: that is why they are contradictories, since one says exactly what the other denies. The same happens with E and I in the shared area.</p>\n\n<h2>10. Checking a syllogism with three circles</h2>\n<p>A syllogism has three terms: the subject of the conclusion (S), the predicate of the conclusion (P) and the middle term (M), which links the premises. Three circles give eight regions. Venn’s method has three steps:</p>\n<p><strong>Figures and moods.</strong> In a syllogism, S is the minor term (the subject of the conclusion), P the major term (the predicate of the conclusion) and M the middle term, which appears in both premises and disappears in the conclusion. The major premise is the one containing P, and the minor premise is the one containing S. Depending on the position occupied by M, four <strong>figures</strong> arise:</p><ul><li>1st: M is subject in the major premise and predicate in the minor premise (M-P, S-M).</li><li>2nd: M is predicate in both (P-M, S-M).</li><li>3rd: M is subject in both (M-P, M-S).</li><li>4th: M is predicate in the major premise and subject in the minor premise (P-M, M-S).</li></ul><p>Aristotle studied the first three in the <em>Prior Analytics</em> (I, 4–6). The fourth was added later. Tradition attributed it to Galen, but that attribution is doubtful.</p><p>Within each figure, the <strong>mood</strong> depends on the type (A, E, I, O) of the major premise, the minor premise and the conclusion. Medieval logicians invented names whose three vowels indicate it: Barbara (AAA) and Ferio (EIO) belong to the first figure. Disamis (IAI) belongs to the third: ‘Some musicians are students; all musicians love rhythm; therefore some who love rhythm are students’. Here M is musicians, P students and S those who love rhythm.</p><p>Checking with Venn. First goes the universal, ‘all M is S’, which empties the part of M that lies outside S. Then the particular, ‘some M is P’. Its ✕ could go in the zone of M and P inside S or outside S, but outside S is already shaded, so it falls inside. That ✕ ends up in S and in P: the conclusion appears without drawing it. The syllogism is valid.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: with the same terms, change the conclusion to ‘all those who love rhythm are students’ (mood IAA). Does the drawing show it? Why does the same content change in validity if the mood changes?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>First draw the universal premises (A and E), which only empty regions.</li>\n<li>Then the particular ones (I and O). If the ✕ could go in two regions and one of them is already empty, it goes in the other; if both are white, it goes on the line between them, because we do not know which one it is in.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not draw the conclusion.</strong> Look at whether the drawing of the premises already shows it. If it does, the syllogism is valid; if it does not, the syllogism is invalid.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P,M;vac=001,101,100,110\"><figcaption><strong>Barbara</strong> (valid). All M are P; all S are M; therefore all S are P. Of S, only the region inside P is left white.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P,M;vac=001,101;x=111\"><figcaption><strong>Datisi</strong> (valid). All M are P; some M are S; therefore some S are P. The ✕ cannot go in the grey region, so it falls inside P.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P,M;vac=010,110;x=100\"><figcaption><strong>Baroco</strong> (valid). All P are M; some S are not M; therefore some S are not P. The ✕ ends up in S and outside P.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P,M;vac=001,101,111\"><figcaption><strong>Not valid</strong>. All M are P; no S are M; therefore no S are P. The region of S inside P and outside M is still white: there may be S that are P.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>The same syllogisms can be checked with <strong>Euler diagrams</strong>, but with a different rule: you have to draw <em>all</em> the situations that the premises allow. If the conclusion holds in all of them, the syllogism is valid; if it fails in even one, it is not. For Barbara one drawing is enough, and it is more intuitive than Venn’s. The invalid syllogism needs two:</p>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=S@150,124,32|M@150,110,58|P@150,100,88,150,34\"><figcaption><strong>Barbara</strong> with Euler: S inside M and M inside P, so S ends up inside P.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=P@110,100,82,110,40|M@96,108,38|S@250,100,40\"><figcaption>Not valid, first situation: all M are P, no S are M… and no S are P. Here the conclusion holds.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=P@150,100,92,150,30|M@112,104,38|S@196,104,34\"><figcaption>Second situation, with the same premises: S is inside P, though outside M. The conclusion fails: the syllogism is not valid.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>The last example commits the error that traditional logic called the <strong>illicit major</strong>: the conclusion speaks about all P, but the premises only say something about part of them. You can try these and other syllogisms in the <a href=\"#logica/silogismos\">Syllogisms</a> tab of the Logic Corner.</p>\n\n<h2>11. Do the S exist? Existential import</h2>\n<p>Go back to the question in section 7: ‘All unicorns have one horn’. In modern logic, A is read as ‘for anything at all, if it is a unicorn, it has one horn’. Since there are no unicorns, there is nothing that makes it false, and the proposition is <strong>true</strong>. That is what the diagram shows: A only empties one region; it does not add any ✕.</p>\n<p>Aristotle reasoned differently. For him, <strong>affirmative</strong> propositions (A and I) assume that the things they speak about exist, and <strong>negative</strong> ones (E and O) do not. His O did not say ‘some S are not P’ but ‘<strong>not all</strong> S are P’, which is true if there are no S. In this way the traditional square is coherent: if there are no unicorns, A and I are false, and E and O are true. All four relations of the square hold.</p>\n<p>Modern logic, since Boole and Venn, does not take for granted that classes have members. The price is that only the <strong>contradictories</strong> survive from the square. The other relations are lost. For example, ‘all unicorns have one horn’ no longer implies ‘some unicorn has one horn’.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> the syllogism <strong>Darapti</strong> (all M are P; all M are S; therefore some S are P) was valid in Aristotelian logic, but it is not valid in modern logic.</p>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P,M;vac=001,101,011\"><figcaption>Darapti without assuming that there are any M: no ✕ appears, and the conclusion ‘some S are P’ does not follow.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=S,P,M;vac=001,101,011;x=111\"><figcaption>If we add ‘there is at least one M’, the ✕ can only go in the centre, and the conclusion appears.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>Neither of the two logics is wrong: they answer different questions. The question to ask about every piece of reasoning is whether we are taking for granted that the thing we are talking about exists. ‘Everyone who hands in the assignment late will lose a point’ is true even if nobody hands it in late. That proposition does not claim that anyone will be late.</p>\n\n<h2>12. Fallacies</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-59\">A <strong>fallacy</strong> is an argument that <strong>seems valid but is not</strong>. Recognising them is a defence against deceit and manipulation. They are divided into two groups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Formal fallacies</strong>: the defect lies in the logical <strong>arrangement</strong> (the structure is not correct).</li>\n<li><strong>Informal fallacies</strong>: the fault resides in <strong>meaning</strong> or in the handling of language. Among the most common are: <strong>ad hominem</strong> (disqualifying the person rather than their reasoning), <strong>ad populum</strong> (invoking that ‘the majority already accepts it’), <strong>ad verecundiam</strong> (resorting to an inappropriate authority), <strong>false cause</strong> (taking coincidence for a causal link), <strong>straw man</strong> (distorting the other person's position in order to defeat it easily), <strong>hasty generalisation</strong> and <strong>slippery slope</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Other informal fallacies</strong>: <strong>ad ignorantiam</strong> (considering something true because no one has demonstrated its falsity, or the reverse), <strong>equivocation</strong> (using the same term with two different meanings within a piece of reasoning; Aristotle included it among the fallacies related to language) and the <strong>fallacy fallacy</strong> or <em>ad logicam</em> (inferring that a thesis is false because the argument that supported it was deficient). If a fallacy is employed deliberately to deceive, it is called a <strong>sophism</strong>; if it is committed involuntarily, a <strong>paralogism</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some important fallacies are studied at greater length in other sections of the website. Here is the gist of each one, with the link to follow up:</p>\n<ul><li><a href=\"#diccionario/g-fil-peticion-de-principio\"><strong>Begging the question</strong></a> (or circular reasoning): the conclusion is already hidden among the premises. ‘This newspaper tells the truth, because the newspaper itself says so.’ The curious thing is that the form is valid (from ‘p’ follows ‘p’); the flaw is that it offers no reason that does not depend on what was to be proved.</li><li><a href=\"#diccionario/g-fil-falso-dilema\"><strong>False dilemma</strong></a>: two ways out are presented as if they were the only ones. ‘Either you pass everything in June or you have lost the year.’ The reasoning that follows is often valid (either A or B; not A; so B); what fails is the premise that cuts the options down to two.</li><li><a href=\"#diccionario/g-fil-ad-hominem\"><strong>Ad hominem</strong></a>, in its forms: the <strong>abusive</strong> one discredits the person (‘what would he know?’); the <strong>circumstantial</strong> one suggests that a thesis is false because of the interests of whoever defends it (‘he only says that because that company pays him’). An interest is a good reason to check the evidence more carefully, but it does not refute it: you have to look at it.</li><li><a href=\"#diccionario/g-fil-tu-quoque\"><strong>Tu quoque</strong></a> (‘you too’): a variant of the <em>ad hominem</em> that answers a criticism by pointing out that the critic does not live up to it either. A doctor who recommends eight hours of sleep and sleeps five may be inconsistent, but that does not make her advice false.</li><li><a href=\"#diccionario/g-fil-apelacion-a-la-autoridad\"><strong>Appeal to authority</strong></a>: it is not always fallacious. Trusting an expert is reasonable when they speak about their own field, when they have no interest that might bend their judgement and when the experts in that field agree. It is a fallacy when the authority speaks about something else (a footballer recommending a medical treatment) or when an expert is quoted while hiding that most of their colleagues think the opposite.</li><li><a href=\"#diccionario/g-fil-causa-falsa\"><strong>False cause</strong></a>: besides the <em>post hoc</em> (‘after this, therefore because of this’), it is worth looking for a <strong>common cause</strong>. On the days when most ice creams are sold there are more cases of sunburn, and not because ice cream burns: both are caused by the summer heat.</li><li><a href=\"#logica/falacias\"><strong>Formal fallacies with a name</strong></a>: <strong>affirming the consequent</strong> (‘if it rains, the ground gets wet; the ground is wet; so it has rained’, when perhaps someone has just watered it) and <strong>denying the antecedent</strong> (‘if it rains, the ground gets wet; it is not raining; so the ground is dry’). You can spot them because a single counterexample is enough to bring them down. The Logic Corner has them all, including those of the syllogism.</li><li><a href=\"#logica/paradojas/sorites\"><strong>The heap (sorites)</strong></a>: one grain of wheat is not a heap, and adding one grain to something that is not a heap does not turn it into a heap; so no number of grains makes a heap. It is not a fallacy but a paradox of vagueness, yet it is related to the slippery slope: both advance in steps so small that none of them seems decisive.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why we reason badly: cognitive biases</h3>\n<p>Fallacies are defects of arguments. But many errors happen before there is any argument at all: in the way the mind perceives, remembers and judges. Psychology calls a <strong>systematic</strong> deviation of judgement a <a href=\"#diccionario/g-fil-sesgo-cognitivo\"><strong>cognitive bias</strong></a>: not an occasional slip, but an error that almost all of us make in the same direction and under the same conditions. It comes from the <strong>economy of thought</strong>. Reasoning carefully is slow and tiring, and for almost everything we do each day a quick, good-enough answer will do. Herbert Simon (1956-1957) spoke of <strong>bounded rationality</strong>: we do not look for the best possible option, but for the first one that satisfies us. The shortcuts with which we manage this are called <strong>heuristics</strong>: simple rules that are almost always right and that, in certain cases, always fail in the same way.</p>\n<p><strong>The five tasks of the orator.</strong> Latin rhetoric divided the work of preparing a speech into five phases. They appear in a youthful work by Cicero, <em>De inventione</em>, in his dialogue <em>De oratore</em> (55 BC) and in the <em>Rhetorica ad Herennium</em>, an anonymous manual from the same period that for centuries was attributed to Cicero:</p><ol><li><em>Inventio</em>: finding the arguments and materials that serve the cause.</li><li><em>Dispositio</em>: ordering them. In basic terms: an exordium, which gains attention; a development, with the statement of facts, the proofs and the refutation of the opponent; and a peroration, which summarises and moves to action.</li><li><em>Elocutio</em>: giving them form with words, choosing the style and the figures.</li><li><em>Memoria</em>: retaining the speech. The <em>Rhetorica ad Herennium</em> teaches how to place ideas mentally in the locations of a familiar building.</li><li><em>Pronuntiatio</em> or <em>actio</em>: voice, gesture and gaze.</li></ol><p>Let us apply it to a class debate on mobile phones in the classroom, arguing that they should be allowed with limits. In <em>inventio</em> you gather reasons (consulting a dictionary, noting down tasks), some data and the foreseeable objections, such as distractions. In <em>dispositio</em> you decide to begin with a situation everyone knows, to present your reasons from the weakest to the strongest, to respond to the objection about distractions and to close with a concrete proposal. In <em>elocutio</em> you look for clear sentences and an example that will be remembered. For <em>memoria</em>, you prepare an outline of three words instead of reading. In <em>actio</em>, you look at the audience and do not rush.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: which of the five phases carries most weight in a good oral presentation? Can a brilliant <em>actio</em> save a poor <em>inventio</em>, or only conceal it?</p>\n<p>The systematic study of these shortcuts was undertaken by two Israeli psychologists, Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman. In an article they published in the journal <em>Science</em> in 1974 they described three of them:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Representativeness</strong>: we judge how likely something is by how much it resembles a typical case, and we forget how many cases there are of each kind. If you ask whether someone who has played the violin since childhood and never misses a concert is more likely to be studying at a conservatoire or studying nursing, most people say the conservatoire, although there are far more nursing students than conservatoire students.</li><li><strong>Availability</strong>: we judge how frequent something is by how easily examples come to mind. After seeing several news reports about a plane crash, flying seems more dangerous than driving, although per kilometre travelled far more people die on the roads.</li><li><strong>Anchoring</strong>: a figure in front of us, even if it has nothing to do with the matter, drags our estimate towards it. In the 1974 experiment a wheel of fortune rigged to land on 10 or 65 was spun, and people were then asked to estimate the percentage of African countries in the United Nations: the median answer of those who had seen 10 was 25%, and that of those who had seen 65 was 45%.</li></ul>\n<p>Years later, Kahneman summed up these ideas (<em>Thinking, Fast and Slow</em>, 2011) with the image of two ways of thinking: a <strong>System 1</strong>, fast, automatic and effortless, which supplies impressions, and a <strong>System 2</strong>, slow, deliberate and costly, which calculates and checks. Biases appear when System 1 answers and System 2, out of laziness or haste, accepts the answer as good. An example by Shane Frederick (2005): a bat and a ball cost 1.10 euros together, and the bat costs one euro more than the ball. How much does the ball cost? The answer that jumps out is 10 cents, and it is wrong: the bat would then cost 1.10 and the total would be 1.20. The ball costs 5 cents.</p>\n<p>Something less innocent is added to the shortcuts: very often we do not reason in order to find out, but in order to get where we already wanted to go. Ziva Kunda (1990) called it <strong>motivated reasoning</strong>, and Thomas Gilovich (1991) summed it up in two questions: faced with a conclusion we like, we ask little (‘can it be true?’); faced with one we dislike, a lot (‘must it be true?’). Leon Festinger had observed it in an extreme case. A small group announced the end of the world for a particular date; when the date passed without incident, the group did not break up, but became convinced that its faith had saved the world and set out to find followers (<em>When Prophecy Fails</em>, 1956). For Festinger, the discomfort of holding two ideas that clash, <strong>cognitive dissonance</strong>, is relieved more readily by changing the interpretation of the facts than by changing the belief. This is what section 21 calls rationalising.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Bias</th><th>What it consists of</th><th>An example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confirmation</td><td>Seeking, remembering and interpreting data so that they bear out what we already think</td><td>Peter Wason (1960) gave the sequence 2-4-6 and asked people to discover the rule. Almost everyone tried sequences that confirmed their hypothesis (8-10-12) and almost no one tried one that could refute it. The rule was ‘any increasing sequence’.</td></tr><tr><td>Hindsight (‘I knew it’)</td><td>Once the outcome is known, believing that it could have been foreseen (Baruch Fischhoff, 1975)</td><td>After the match, the defeat ‘was there for all to see’.</td></tr><tr><td>Fundamental attribution error</td><td>Explaining other people’s behaviour by their character and forgetting the situation (Lee Ross, 1977)</td><td>A serious waiter strikes us as unfriendly, without our thinking that he may be ten hours into his shift.</td></tr><tr><td>Self-serving</td><td>Taking the credit for successes and blaming failures on circumstances</td><td>‘I passed because I studied; I failed because the exam was unfair.’</td></tr><tr><td>False consensus</td><td>Believing that others think as we do more than they really do (Ross, Greene and House, 1977)</td><td>Someone who shares an opinion with their group of friends believes it is ‘everyone’s’.</td></tr><tr><td>Bias blind spot</td><td>Seeing biases in others far more than in oneself (Emily Pronin, 2002)</td><td>Reading this table while thinking of other people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>A bias and a fallacy are not the same thing.</strong> A fallacy is a defect of an <strong>argument</strong>: it is in plain view, in what someone says or writes, and you can point to the premise that fails. A bias is a <strong>tendency of the mind</strong>: it acts before there is any argument, often without our noticing, and it is not corrected just by knowing logic. They are related. A bias often pushes us towards a fallacy (whoever looks only for confirmations ends up in a hasty generalisation), and a fallacy convinces because it rests on a bias (the <em>ad populum</em> exploits our tendency to follow the group). In short: the fallacy is in the text; the bias, in whoever writes or reads it.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> not every error is a bias. Getting something wrong because data are missing is ignorance, and it is fixed with more information. A bias is an error that repeats itself in the same direction even when the data are in front of us; that is why informing people is not enough: the way of looking has to change.</p>\n<p>The suspicion is not new. Francis Bacon warned in the <em>Novum Organum</em> (1620, book I, aphorism 46) that the human understanding, once it has adopted an opinion, draws everything else in to support it, and that contrary cases, even if they are more numerous and weightier, it overlooks or despises. He illustrates it with an ancient anecdote: a man was shown in a temple the pictures offered by those who had escaped shipwreck after making a vow to the gods, and he asked where the pictures were of those who had made the vow and drowned. It is confirmation bias three centuries before it had a name. Bacon counted it among the <strong>idols of the tribe</strong>, the errors that come from human nature itself and not from each individual. The text is in the reading <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-descartes-bacon\">‘Descartes and Bacon: doubt and the idols’</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>What is rhetoric for?</strong> At the beginning of the <em>Rhetoric</em> (I, 1, 1355a-b), Aristotle defends its usefulness with several reasons. The first: the true and the just are by nature stronger than their opposites, so that if in a trial what should prevail does not, the fault lies with those who did not know how to defend it. The second: before certain audiences not even the most exact science is persuasive, because teaching with science requires a training that not everyone has; therefore one must persuade starting from the common notions shared by the audience. The third: it is useful to know how to argue in favour of both positions, not in order to defend both, but in order to see how things are and to refute someone who argues unjustly.</p><p>Aristotle knows that rhetoric can serve contrary ends and that, badly used, it does great harm. He replies that this happens with all goods except virtue, such as strength, health or wealth: well used they help greatly, and badly used they harm greatly. The skill is the same; what makes it good or bad is its use, and it must serve what is just.</p><p>Plato, his teacher, had been harsher. In the <em>Gorgias</em> (462-465) he makes Socrates say that rhetoric is not an art (<em>tékhnē</em>) but a form of flattery that seeks to please and not the good. It would be to justice what cookery is to medicine: it flatters the palate but does not cure.</p><p>Think of a duty solicitor who, without choosing it, has to defend an innocent defendant without resources. If she orders the facts well, finds the evidence and knows how to present it, she prevents an injustice. There rhetoric protects the weak.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is a technique that serves both good and evil ethically neutral? Or are there techniques that, by the way they work, already push towards a particular use?</p>\n<p>Does all this mean that we are irrational? Not necessarily. Gerd Gigerenzer has argued that many heuristics are <strong>rational in their environment</strong>: ‘fast and frugal’ rules which, with little information and little time, are right as often as a complicated calculation, or more (<em>Simple Heuristics That Make Us Smart</em>, 1999). The problem appears when the shortcut is used outside the ground where it works: with statistics, with rare risks or in an environment designed to take advantage of it, such as advertising or social media. Underneath lies a philosophical question: what does being rational mean — always being right, or deciding well with what you have?</p>\n<p>What can be done? Knowing that biases exist helps little on its own, because the blind spot makes us see them mostly in others. Some habits work better: <strong>considering the opposite</strong>, that is, asking what reasons there would be if the opposite conclusion were the true one (Lord, Lepper and Preston, 1984); deliberately looking for the piece of data that could refute us, as Popper asked; <strong>slowing down</strong> before accepting the answer that seems obvious; and thinking with others, because each person’s biases are easier to see from outside. That is why philosophy values dialogue so highly (section 21) and science values review by other researchers. In <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a>, in the section on post-truth, you will see how fake news and echo chambers take advantage of these shortcuts.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if the mind makes systematic errors, what can we correct it with, if the tool that corrects is the mind itself? Is it enough to be many to make up for each person’s biases, or do groups have their own too?</p>\n\n<h2>13. Connectives and their truth tables</h2>\n<p>Logic combines propositions with <strong>connectives</strong>. The truth value of the result depends only on that of the parts, and is set out in a <strong>truth table</strong> (V = true, F = false):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>p</th><th>q</th><th>¬p (not p)</th><th>p ∧ q (and)</th><th>p ∨ q (or)</th><th>p → q (if… then)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>V</td><td>V</td><td>F</td><td>V</td><td>V</td><td>V</td></tr>\n<tr><td>V</td><td>F</td><td>F</td><td>F</td><td>V</td><td>F</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F</td><td>V</td><td>V</td><td>F</td><td>V</td><td>V</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F</td><td>F</td><td>V</td><td>F</td><td>F</td><td>V</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <strong>conjunction</strong> (∧) is true only if both parts are true; the <strong>disjunction</strong> (∨), if at least one is; the <strong>conditional</strong> (→) is false only when the antecedent is true and the consequent false. These tables let you check whether an argument is <strong>valid</strong>: it is valid if there is no row in which the premises are true and the conclusion false. A formula that is true in every row is a <strong>tautology</strong>; if it is false in every row, a <strong>contradiction</strong>.</p>\n<p>The table for the conditional explains four ways of reasoning that turn up every day. They look very alike, but two are valid and two are not:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Form</th><th>Example</th><th>Valid?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>Modus ponens</em></td><td>p → q; p; so q</td><td>If the ticket has a stamp, entry is free; it has a stamp; so entry is free</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><em>Modus tollens</em></td><td>p → q; ¬q; so ¬p</td><td>If someone had got in, the alarm would have gone off; it has not gone off; so no one has got in</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Affirming the consequent</td><td>p → q; q; so p</td><td>If it is flu, there is a temperature; there is a temperature; so it is flu</td><td>No: a temperature can have other causes</td></tr><tr><td>Denying the antecedent</td><td>p → q; ¬p; so ¬q</td><td>If it is Sunday, the library is closed; it is not Sunday; so it is open</td><td>No: it may close for a bank holiday or for building work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Why the last two fail can be seen in the truth table: there is a row in which the antecedent is false and the consequent true. In that row the conditional and ‘q’ are true, but ‘p’ is false; so when the consequent is affirmed, the premises can be true and the conclusion false. When the antecedent is denied, the same happens in that row. <em>Modus tollens</em>, on the other hand, is the form of refutation: if a hypothesis predicts something and that something does not happen, the hypothesis falls. It is the idea that Popper placed at the centre of science (section 21) and the one behind reductio ad absurdum. You can practise these rules in the <a href=\"#logica/inferencia\">rules of inference</a> in the Logic Corner.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘If you help me move house, I’ll take you out to dinner.’ If you don’t help me, have I promised not to take you out? Logically, no: the conditional only says what happens if you help me. But in conversation almost all of us understand it as a <strong>biconditional</strong> (‘if and only if’), and that is why denying the antecedent seems so natural to us. Before accusing someone of that fallacy it is worth asking what they meant: sometimes the ‘if’ of conversation is an ‘only if’.</p>\n<p>A <strong>paradox</strong> is not the same as a contradiction. For Quine it is a conclusion that seems absurd but has an argument in its favour. One grain of rice is not a heap, and one more grain does not turn into a heap what was not one; repeating the step, not even a whole sack would be one (the <strong>sorites paradox</strong>, from the Greek <em>sōrós</em>, ‘heap’). The most famous is the <strong>liar paradox</strong>, ‘this sentence is false’: taking it as true forces us to hold it to be false, and vice versa, so it cannot coherently be given any truth value. A sophism hides an error in order to persuade; a paradox leaves everything in plain view and forces us to sharpen concepts as basic as ‘heap’ or ‘truth’.</p>\n\n<p>The idea of turning reasoning into a <strong>calculation</strong> is far older than computers. In the 13th century, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/llull\">Ramon Llull</a></strong> (c. 1232-c. 1316) devised in Majorca his <em>Ars Magna</em>: a system of rotating <strong>paper wheels</strong> with basic concepts that, when combined, generated every possible statement about a subject. It was a mechanical “thinking machine”, a first attempt at <strong>combinatorial logic</strong>. Centuries later, Leibniz dreamed of taking that idea further —a language in which to reason would be to calculate—, and from that lineage comes the logic of the next section.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: can all thought be reduced to combining symbols according to rules, as a machine does, or is there something in human reasoning that escapes? Llull is not in the Year 2 syllabus, but you can place him in the <a href=\"#genealogias/len\">“Logic and language”</a> line in Genealogies, from Aristotle to Leibniz and Russell.</p>\n<h2>14. A bridge to computing: Boolean algebra and logic gates</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-69\">In 1854, <strong>George Boole</strong> showed that logic could be treated as an <strong>algebra</strong>: if we write <strong>1</strong> for ‘true’ and <strong>0</strong> for ‘false’, the connectives become operations (conjunction works like a product, disjunction like a sum). This is <strong>Boolean algebra</strong>.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-70\">Almost a century later, <strong>Claude Shannon</strong> (1938) realised that these operations could be built with <strong>electric circuits</strong>: <strong>logic gates</strong> (AND, OR, NOT…). An <strong>AND</strong> gate lets current (1) through only if both its inputs receive it: exactly the truth table of conjunction. In this way the old logic of Aristotle and Frege became <strong>electricity</strong>, and it is today the foundation of every computer: each operation of a processor is, at bottom, logic.</p>\n<p><strong>Proving instead of checking all the rows.</strong> A truth table checks validity by examining all possible cases. With three letters there are 8 rows; with ten there would be 1,024. <strong>Natural deduction</strong>, which logicians systematised in the mid-1930s, follows a different path. It advances from the premises to the conclusion by small steps, and each step is authorised by a valid rule of inference. The method is this:</p><ol><li>Number the premises.</li><li>Add new lines, and on each one note the rule used and the lines from which it follows.</li><li>The proof ends when the conclusion appears.</li></ol><p>The basic rules are these:</p><ul><li><strong>Modus ponens</strong> (MP): from p → q and p, q follows.</li><li><strong>Modus tollens</strong> (MT): from p → q and ¬q, ¬p follows.</li><li><strong>Hypothetical syllogism</strong> (HS): from p → q and q → r, p → r follows.</li><li><strong>Disjunctive syllogism</strong> (DS): from p ∨ q and ¬p, q follows.</li><li><strong>Simplification</strong> (Simp): from p ∧ q, p follows.</li><li><strong>Conjunction</strong> (Conj): from p and q, p ∧ q follows.</li></ul><p>An example, with p = it rains, q = the playground gets wet and r = PE is cancelled:</p><ol><li>p → q (premise)</li><li>q → r (premise)</li><li>p (premise)</li><li>q (MP 1, 3)</li><li>r (MP 2, 4)</li></ol><p>There is another equally correct route: 4. p → r (HS 1, 2); 5. r (MP 4, 3). A proof is not unique; what matters is that each line is justified. If a single line is not, the chain breaks, even though the conclusion is true.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what advantage does a proof have compared with testing all the cases? And what drawback does it have? If you cannot find the proof, have you proved that the argument is not valid?</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Logic</th><th>Boolean algebra</th><th>Logic gate</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>¬p (negation)</td><td>NOT: 1→0, 0→1</td><td>NOT</td></tr>\n<tr><td>p ∧ q (conjunction)</td><td>product: 1·1 = 1</td><td>AND</td></tr>\n<tr><td>p ∨ q (disjunction)</td><td>sum: 1 + 0 = 1</td><td>OR</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2>15. Classes and sets</h2>\n<p>Boole did not apply his algebra only to propositions: he also designed it for <strong>classes</strong>. Behind ‘all mammals are vertebrates’ there are two groups: mammals and vertebrates. Logic calls them <strong>classes</strong>, and mathematics calls them <strong>sets</strong>. A class can be given in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>By <strong>extension</strong>, listing its members: {Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday}.</li>\n<li>By <strong>comprehension</strong>, stating the property they share: {x | x is a weekday}. It is the same class described in a different way.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There exists a class that entirely lacks elements, the <strong>empty class</strong> (∅): the one formed by unicorns or by even numbers greater than 2 that are also prime. And we call <strong>universe</strong> (U) the set of everything that is spoken about at a given moment: the pupils of the school, numbers, living beings…</p>\n<p>Two relations look the same but are not:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Membership</strong> (∈): an individual is a member of a class. ‘Socrates ∈ humans’.</li>\n<li><strong>Inclusion</strong> (⊆): one class is inside another. ‘Humans ⊆ mortals’: every member of the first is a member of the second.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\"><figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-euler=\"c=humans@140,118,48,140,94|mortals@150,100,92,150,32;p=Socrates@140,130,140,156\"><figcaption>An <strong>Euler diagram</strong>: the class of humans is inside the class of mortals (inclusion, ⊆); Socrates is a point inside the first (membership, ∈).</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> in everyday language both are expressed with ‘is’ (‘Socrates is human’, ‘a human is mortal’), but they do not have the same form. Inclusion can be chained: if humans ⊆ mortals and mortals ⊆ living beings, then humans ⊆ living beings. Membership cannot: Socrates is a member of the class of humans, and the class of humans is a member of the class of species, but Socrates is not a species.</p>\n\n<h2>16. Operations with classes</h2>\n<p>In <em>The Mathematical Analysis of Logic</em> (1847) and <em>The Laws of Thought</em> (1854), Boole showed that we can calculate with classes almost as we do with numbers; later Ernst Schröder (1841-1902) developed this ‘algebra of logic’ (1890). These are the basic operations. <strong>Mind the colour code:</strong> in these drawings the <strong>colour</strong> marks the region that results from the operation; it does not mean ‘empty’, unlike the grey in sections 9 and 10.</p>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B;sel=10,11,01\"><figcaption><strong>Union</strong> A ∪ B: what is in A, in B or in both. It corresponds to ‘or’ (∨).</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B;sel=11\"><figcaption><strong>Intersection</strong> A ∩ B: what is in both A and B at the same time. It corresponds to ‘and’ (∧).</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B;sel=10\"><figcaption><strong>Difference</strong> A − B: what is in A but not in B.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B;sel=00,01\"><figcaption><strong>Complement</strong> Aᶜ: everything in the universe that is not in A. It corresponds to ‘not’ (¬).</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B;sel=10,01\"><figcaption><strong>Symmetric difference</strong>: what is in one of the two, but not in both. It corresponds to exclusive ‘or’.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>Example: if A = {1, 2, 3} and B = {3, 4, 5}, then A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, A ∩ B = {3} and A − B = {1, 2}. If two classes have nothing in common (A ∩ B = ∅), they are called <strong>disjoint</strong>.</p>\n<p>The correspondence with the connectives in section 13 is no coincidence: ‘x ∈ A ∪ B’ means ‘x ∈ A <strong>or</strong> x ∈ B’. That is why the same laws hold for classes and for truth tables.</p>\n\n<h2>17. The laws of classes, checked with drawings</h2>\n<p><strong>Where the parentheses go.</strong> When speaking, tone and pauses help us understand how the parts of a sentence are grouped; in a formula, parentheses do that. Without them, p ∧ q → r could be read in two ways. For that reason a convention is followed. Negation affects only what it immediately precedes. Conjunction and disjunction bind more tightly. The conditional and the biconditional have <strong>greater scope</strong>: they are usually the <strong>main connective</strong>, the one that divides the formula into its two large parts. Thus, p ∧ q → r is read as (p ∧ q) → r.</p><p>With p = I study, q = I rest and r = I pass:</p><ul><li>(p ∧ q) → r: ‘If I study and rest, I pass’. It is a conditional, and it does not say whether I will study.</li><li>p ∧ (q → r): ‘I study, and if I rest, I pass’. Here it is affirmed that I study.</li></ul><p>The truth table shows this. In the four rows in which p is true, the two formulae coincide, because both have the same value as q → r. In the four rows in which p is false, they separate. The first is always true, because its antecedent is false and a conditional with a false antecedent is true. The second is always false, because a conjunction with one false part is false. If I do not study, the first sentence remains true; the second was false from the start.</p><p>Formalising well therefore requires deciding first which is the main connective, that is, the word that organises the whole sentence.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: formalise ‘I am not going if it rains or it is cold’ in two ways, with p = it rains, q = it is cold and r = I go: (p ∨ q) → ¬r and p ∨ (q → ¬r). Which one reflects what is meant? What does the other assert?</p>\n<p>Operations with classes obey laws that allow us to turn one expression into another, equivalent one. A law is checked by drawing both sides of the equation: if the colour falls in exactly the same regions, the law holds.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>For union</th><th>For intersection</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Commutative</td><td>A ∪ B = B ∪ A</td><td>A ∩ B = B ∩ A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Associative</td><td>(A ∪ B) ∪ C = A ∪ (B ∪ C)</td><td>(A ∩ B) ∩ C = A ∩ (B ∩ C)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Identity</td><td>A ∪ ∅ = A</td><td>A ∩ U = A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Domination</td><td>A ∪ U = U</td><td>A ∩ ∅ = ∅</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Complement</td><td>A ∪ Aᶜ = U</td><td>A ∩ Aᶜ = ∅</td></tr>\n<tr><td>De Morgan</td><td>(A ∪ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ</td><td>(A ∩ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∪ Bᶜ</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Distributive</td><td>A ∪ (B ∩ C) = (A ∪ B) ∩ (A ∪ C)</td><td>A ∩ (B ∪ C) = (A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>De Morgan’s laws</strong>, named after the logician Augustus De Morgan (1806-1871), are the ones used most often in reasoning: denying ‘A or B’ is the same as saying ‘neither A nor B’, and denying ‘A and B’ is saying ‘not A, or not B’.</p>\n<div class=\"tv-venns\">\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B;sel=00\"><figcaption>(A ∪ B)ᶜ: outside the union.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B;sel=00\"><figcaption>Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ: outside A and, at the same time, outside B. It is the same region.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B,C;sel=110,101,111\"><figcaption>A ∩ (B ∪ C): the part of A that touches B or C.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"tv-venn\" data-venn=\"c=A,B,C;sel=110,101,111\"><figcaption>(A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C): the same region. The distributive law holds.</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<p>These laws can also be used for counting: the number of elements in a union is |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|. The intersection is subtracted because, when A and B are added together, its elements have been counted twice. If in a class there are 12 people who play football, 9 who play basketball and 4 who play both, the number who play at least one of them is 12 + 9 − 4 = 17.</p>\n\n<h2>18. Not every property forms a class: Russell’s paradox</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-92\">So far it seemed that any property defines a class: the class of red things, the class of even numbers… In 1901 Bertrand Russell found a problem. Some classes are members of themselves (the class of things that are not cats is not a cat, so it contains itself), and others are not (the class of cats is not a cat). Think of the class R of <strong>all classes that are not members of themselves</strong>. Is R a member of R?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If it is, it has the property that defines R, so it is not a member of itself.</li>\n<li>If it is not, it has the property that defines R, so it is a member of R.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Either way we reach a contradiction. Russell told Gottlob Frege about it in a letter dated 16 June 1902, when Frege was about to publish the second volume of his great work on the foundations of arithmetic, which relied precisely on this idea of class. Frege added an appendix acknowledging the problem (1903). The way out that prevailed was to lay down rules (axioms) fixing which collections can be sets, instead of accepting any property whatever. You will find a version of this paradox, the barber, in the worksheet <a href=\"#logica/paradojas/barbero\">Russell’s barber</a>.</p>\n\n<p>The symbolic logic that Frege and Russell built did not stay within mathematics. Already in the 20th century, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/carnap\">Rudolf Carnap</a></strong> (1891-1970), of the <strong>Vienna Circle</strong>, proposed using it as a tool for philosophy itself: the philosopher’s task would not be to build great systems about the world, but to <strong>analyse language</strong> with the rigour of logic —that of the connectives and Boole’s algebra of sections 13 and 14— to clarify what each statement really says. According to his <strong>verification criterion</strong>, a statement has meaning if it can be checked against experience or is true by pure logic; whatever meets neither —many claims of metaphysics— would not be false, but <strong>meaningless</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>In the domain of metaphysics, logical analysis has led to the negative result that the alleged statements of that domain are entirely meaningless. — Rudolf Carnap, “The Elimination of Metaphysics through Logical Analysis of Language” (1932)</p> </blockquote>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: does only what can be checked with facts or proved by logic really have meaning? Where would ethics, aesthetics or Carnap’s own sentence stand then? It is an <strong>open debate</strong> that you will continue in Year 2, when you work on analytic philosophy; you can place Carnap in the <a href=\"#genealogias/len\">“Logic and language”</a> line in Genealogies.</p>\n<h2>19. Beyond deduction and induction: the best explanation and analogy</h2>\n<p>In section 4 we met <strong>abduction</strong>, or inference to the best explanation. One question remains: what makes an explanation ‘the best’? There is no mechanical rule, but several <strong>criteria</strong> are usually applied: it should account for <strong>all the data</strong> (not just the data that suit us), it should be <strong>coherent</strong> with what we already know, it should be <strong>simple</strong> (not multiplying assumptions unnecessarily, the principle known as <strong>Ockham’s razor</strong>), and it should allow <strong>testable predictions</strong>. If your phone switches off at 30% battery every time it is cold, ‘the battery is old and the cold affects it’ explains more with less than ‘a different thing goes wrong every day’.</p>\n<p>The three modes of reasoning are usually <strong>chained together</strong>. Your phone has been plugged in all night and is still at 5 %. Of the possible causes (the socket, the battery, the cable) you choose the most plausible, the cable, which was already frayed (<strong>abduction</strong>). Then you work out what should happen if you are right: with your sister’s charger, the phone will charge (<strong>deduction</strong>). You repeat the test with several devices, and each result that fits increases your confidence by <strong>induction</strong>, without turning it into certainty. This is how Peirce saw inquiry: making a conjecture, deriving consequences and letting experience judge the hypothesis.</p>\n<p>Another very common form of reasoning is <strong>analogy</strong>: two things are alike in several respects; one of them also has a certain property; therefore the other probably has it too. This is how we reason when a medicine is tested on mice before people, or when a court decides a new case by relying on a similar one. An analogy never guarantees anything; its <strong>strength</strong> depends on the similarities being <strong>relevant</strong> to the conclusion and on there being no <strong>decisive differences</strong>. When it fails on this count we speak of a <strong>false analogy</strong>: ‘a country is like a family, so it should be governed as a father governs his children’ overlooks the fact that citizens are adults, not children.</p>\n<p>A classic example: <strong>William Paley</strong> (<em>Natural Theology</em>, 1802) compared living beings—with the eye as the star example—to a watch found on a heath: if a watch requires a watchmaker, such a finely adjusted organ would require a designer. Earlier, <strong>Hume</strong> (<em>Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion</em>, 1779) had already pointed out the weaknesses of this kind of analogy, and later Darwin offered another explanation for the fit of living beings to their environment. Whether the argument convinces or not is still debated, but it shows clearly that <strong>an analogy is worth as much as the resemblance in what matters</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>20. How to analyse a real argument</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-104\">The arguments of everyday life do not arrive neatly arranged into premises and conclusion. <strong>Indicators</strong> help in analysing them: words that signal the function of each sentence. ‘Therefore’, ‘so’, ‘hence’, ‘thus’ or ‘consequently’ indicate a <strong>conclusion</strong>; ‘because’, ‘since’, ‘for’ or ‘given that’ indicate a <strong>premise</strong>. Careful: ‘because’ does not always introduce an argument. ‘The glass broke because a stone fell on it’ is an <strong>explanation</strong> (nobody doubts that it broke; we are saying why), not an argument (which tries to convince us of something that is under discussion).</p>\n<p>Moreover, almost all real arguments have <strong>implicit premises</strong>: assumptions that are not stated because they are taken as obvious. An argument with an unexpressed premise is traditionally called an <strong>enthymeme</strong>. Bringing these premises to light is the most useful part of the analysis, because the weak point is often exactly there, and many discussions get stuck because each side takes a different thing for granted without saying so.</p>\n<p><strong>Three dimensions of the message.</strong> The American philosopher Charles Morris, in <em>Foundations of the Theory of Signs</em> (1938), proposed studying signs from three points of view:</p><ul><li><strong>Syntax</strong>: the relation of signs to one another, that is, the rules for combining them correctly. Noam Chomsky gave a famous example in <em>Syntactic Structures</em> (1957): “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” (“colourless green ideas sleep furiously”). The sentence is well formed, but it makes no sense: ideas do not have colour and do not sleep.</li><li><strong>Semantics</strong>: the relation between signs and what they mean or that to which they refer. Here the question is what “bank” means or to whom “the current mayoress” refers.</li><li><strong>Pragmatics</strong>: the relation between signs and those who use them, with their context and their intention.</li></ul><p>“Can you close the window?” is a question by its syntax, and by its literal sense it asks about your ability. But in a cold classroom nobody answers “yes, I can” and stays still: pragmatically, it is a request. Someone who responds only to the literal sense does not fail in grammar, but in the situation.</p><p>The three dimensions help to locate misunderstandings when arguing. Ambiguity can be syntactic (“I saw your cousin with the binoculars”: who was carrying them?), semantic (equivocation plays on two senses of the same word) or pragmatic (two people argue and do not realise that one is informing and the other hears a reproach). Before rebutting, it is worth asking on which of the three levels the disagreement lies.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: when can a correct and meaningful sentence fail pragmatically? Think of a “don’t worry, it’s nothing” said at the wrong moment, or of answering only “yes” to “do you know what time it is?”.</p>\n<p>An implicit premise must not be confused with an <strong>assumption</strong> (also called a working hypothesis): a statement accepted <strong>only to see what follows from it</strong>, without claiming that it is true. ‘Let us suppose that the universe had a beginning…’ does not say that it had one: it opens a line of reasoning which, at the end, stops depending on that assumption or rejects it. This is how reductio ad absurdum (section 21) and conditional proof in natural deduction work; in the <a href=\"#logica/deduccion\">Logic Corner</a> it is done by opening and closing assumptions. What is not allowed is to forget that it was an assumption and to present at the end as proved what had only been assumed.</p>\n<p>A worked example, step by step. Text: ‘You shouldn’t trust that health website, since it doesn’t say who writes it.’</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Find the conclusion</strong> (what you are meant to accept): ‘you shouldn’t trust that website’. The indicator ‘since’ marks what follows as a premise.</li>\n<li><strong>Write down the explicit premises</strong>: P1. That website does not say who writes it.</li>\n<li><strong>Add the implicit premise</strong> needed for the conclusion to follow: P2. You should not trust websites that do not say who writes them.</li>\n<li><strong>Draw the outline</strong>: P1 + P2, therefore C. With P2 added, the argument is <strong>valid</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluate the premises</strong>: P1 is checked by looking at the website. P2, as it stands, is more debatable: there are collective works with no visible author that are reliable, and signed websites that are not. A more reasonable version would be ‘a health website that does not say who writes it or what it is based on deserves <strong>less</strong> trust’. With it, the conclusion is no longer ‘never trust it’ but ‘check it against another source’.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Of <strong>non-deductive</strong> arguments we do not ask whether they are valid, but how much <strong>strength</strong> they have. Some guiding questions: is the sample <strong>large and varied</strong>? Are there known <strong>counterexamples</strong>? Have <strong>other explanations</strong> been ruled out? Are the similarities relevant? <strong>To analyse an argument is to reconstruct it in full, hidden premises included, before judging it.</strong></p>\n\n<h2>21. Arguing in a dialogue: the philosopher’s tools</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-110\">Arguing is not just chaining premises together on your own: it is almost always done <strong>with others</strong>, and philosophy has refined a few rules and tools so that discussion serves to think better and not just to win.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Burden of proof</strong>: whoever makes a claim has to give reasons for it, and the further it departs from what is well established, the more reasons it needs. If someone claims that a magnetic bracelet cures anxiety, it is up to them to prove it; it is not up to you to show that it does not work.</li>\n<li><strong>Principle of charity</strong>: before criticising, interpret the other person’s position in its <strong>strongest and most reasonable version</strong>, the one they themselves would recognise as faithful. It is exactly the opposite of the straw man, and a good test is to summarise their argument until they say ‘yes, that is what I think’.</li>\n<li><strong>Counterexample</strong>: to refute a <strong>universal</strong> claim (‘all…’, ‘never…’, ‘whenever…’) a single case that goes against it is enough. A thousand white swans do not prove that all swans are white; one black swan proves that they are not. That is why <strong>Karl Popper</strong> argued that science advances by looking for what could refute its hypotheses (<strong>falsificationism</strong>), and not merely by accumulating confirmations.</li>\n<li><strong>Reductio ad absurdum</strong>: the thesis one wants to reject is supposed, for the sake of examining it, and it is shown that a contradiction or something clearly unacceptable follows from it; therefore it must be abandoned. Suppose there is a natural number greater than all the others, N. But N + 1 is also a natural number and is greater than N: contradiction. Hence there is no greatest natural number.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Another danger deserves watching: <strong>rationalising</strong> instead of <strong>reasoning</strong>. Reasoning is going from the premises to the conclusion; rationalising is starting from the conclusion we already wanted (because it suits us or we like it) and then looking for tailor-made premises. Someone who rationalises is not prepared to change their mind; someone who reasons is. It is also worth separating what can be discussed with reasons and evidence from <strong>personal tastes</strong>: preferring summer to winter cannot be refuted with arguments (moral judgements, on the other hand, can be discussed with reasons, as you will see in ethics).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th>Mistake it helps to avoid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Burden of proof</td><td>Decides who must provide the reasons</td><td>Ad ignorantiam fallacy</td></tr><tr><td>Principle of charity</td><td>Interprets the other person in their best version</td><td>Straw man</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexample</td><td>Refutes a universal claim with a single case</td><td>Hasty generalisation</td></tr><tr><td>Reductio ad absurdum</td><td>Rejects a thesis by showing that it leads to a contradiction</td><td>Accepting incoherent theses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In the <em>Gorgias</em>, Plato has Socrates say that he prefers to be refuted rather than to refute, because freeing oneself from an error is a greater good than freeing someone else from it. <strong>In a philosophical dialogue we argue in order to get closer to the truth, and that requires being prepared to be refuted.</strong></p>\n\n<p>This trust in reasoned dialogue has a medieval root. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/abelardo\">Peter Abelard</a></strong> (1079-1142) made <strong>dialectic</strong> —the art of reasoning and arguing— the central method of philosophy. In his work <em>Sic et Non</em> (“Yes and No”) he gathered 158 questions and, for each one, quotations from Christian authorities that contradicted one another; instead of hiding the clash, he set it out in the open to force thinking. His motto sums it up: <strong>by doubting we come to ask, and by asking we reach the truth</strong>. To argue well is not, for Abelard, to pile up quotations, but to examine the reasons.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: when two respected authorities contradict each other, is it enough to pick the one you like best, or must you ask both for their reasons? You will meet Abelard again in Year 2 in medieval philosophy, alongside the problem of universals.</p>\n<h2>22. Defining and distinguishing</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t4-118\">Many discussions are, at bottom, disagreements about <strong>what a word means</strong>. That is why a basic tool of the philosopher is to <strong>define</strong> precisely. The classical definition, of Aristotelian origin, states the <strong>proximate genus</strong> (the broader class to which something belongs) and the <strong>specific difference</strong> (what distinguishes it within that class): a square is a <em>rectangle</em> (genus) <em>with four equal sides</em> (difference). A good definition must not be <strong>circular</strong> (‘freedom is being free’), nor <strong>too broad</strong> (‘the human being is an animal that lives in groups’: so do ants), nor <strong>too narrow</strong> (‘a sport is a game with a ball’: it leaves out swimming), nor more <strong>obscure</strong> than what it defines.</p>\n<p>As useful as defining is <strong>distinguishing</strong> concepts that get confused. ‘Illegal’ and ‘immoral’ do not mean the same: cheating in an exam is not a crime, but it is wrong; and the laws that in the past imposed racial segregation were legal and unjust. Many discussions dissolve as soon as someone says: ‘let’s make a distinction’.</p>\n<p>For defining and for reasoning, it is key to separate two types of condition:</p>\n<p><strong>Three ways of persuading.</strong> In the <em>Rhetoric</em> (I, 2, 1356a), Aristotle distinguishes three types of proof provided by the speech itself:</p><ul><li><em>Ēthos</em>: the character of the speaker. The person who convinces is someone worthy of trust. Aristotle specifies that this trust must arise from the speech itself, from how the speaker speaks and reasons, and not from any reputation he or she had before.</li><li><em>Páthos</em>: the emotional disposition of the audience. We do not judge in the same way when we are happy as when we are sad, nor when we are afraid as when we feel compassion.</li><li><em>Lógos</em>: the reasons and proofs of the speech itself, what shows, or seems to show, that something is the case.</li></ul><p>Imagine a flu vaccination campaign. A family doctor well known in the neighbourhood speaks, explains calmly and acknowledges what is not known (<em>ēthos</em>). The story is told of a grandfather who spent weeks in hospital and of his anguished family (<em>páthos</em>). And data are shown about the hospital admissions that the vaccine prevents each winter (<em>lógos</em>).</p><p>The three paths are often combined, and there is nothing wrong with that: a good reason is more persuasive if it is given by someone reliable and reaches the person listening. But the rational basis is <em>lógos</em>. Without it, <em>ēthos</em> is reduced to “believe me because it is me”, very close to the fallacy of authority, and <em>páthos</em> is reduced to stirring emotions so that people do not think. The enemy is not emotions, but using them instead of reasons.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: choose a current advertisement (for a mobile phone, a drink or an NGO) and look for the three paths in it. Which one dominates? Does it give you any reason, or only a familiar face and an emotion?</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Necessary condition</strong>: without it the other thing does not occur, although on its own it is not enough. Being eighteen is necessary to vote in general elections in Spain, but not sufficient (nationality is also required, among other requirements).</li>\n<li><strong>Sufficient condition</strong>: if it occurs, the other thing certainly occurs, although the latter could also occur in other ways. Being a square is sufficient for being a rectangle, but not necessary: there are rectangles that are not squares.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Relation</th><th>How to read it</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A is necessary for B</td><td>If not A, not B (if B, then A)</td><td>Oxygen for there to be fire</td></tr><tr><td>A is sufficient for B</td><td>If A, then B</td><td>Being a square for being a rectangle</td></tr><tr><td>A is necessary and sufficient for B</td><td>A if and only if B</td><td>Having three sides for being a triangle (among polygons)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>With these notions we practise <strong>conceptual analysis</strong>: conditions are proposed for a concept and put to the test with counterexamples. A worked example: what is it to <strong>lie</strong>?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>First proposal: lying is <strong>saying something false</strong>. Counterexample: someone who states in good faith that the exam is on Monday, when it was actually on Tuesday, has made a mistake but has not lied. Saying something false is not sufficient.</li>\n<li>Second proposal: lying is saying something that <strong>one believes to be false</strong>. Counterexample: an actor on stage or someone telling a joke say things they know to be false, and nobody calls them liars. That is not enough either.</li>\n<li>Third proposal: lying is asserting something that one believes to be false <strong>with the intention that another person believe it</strong>. It withstands the earlier cases better. And it reveals something curious: if you assert in order to deceive something you believe false, but by chance it turns out to be true, according to this definition you have lied all the same. So saying something false is not even necessary.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Philosophers still argue about borderline cases, such as the brazen lie that everyone knows to be a lie, but the method is this. <strong>To define well is to give necessary and sufficient conditions and to test them with counterexamples.</strong></p>\n\n<h2>23. Practice: propositions, classes and diagrams</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Say which type (A, E, I, O) each sentence is: ‘There are honest politicians’; ‘Not all that glitters is gold’; ‘No triangle has four sides’; ‘Dogs are mammals’.</li>\n<li>If ‘all the exams in this course are written’ is false, what do we know about ‘some exam in this course is not written’? And about ‘no exam in this course is written’?</li>\n<li>Draw (A ∩ B)ᶜ and Aᶜ ∪ Bᶜ and check that it is the same region.</li>\n<li>Is this syllogism valid? ‘No reptiles have hair; some pets are reptiles; therefore some pets do not have hair’.</li>\n<li>Find an example from everyday life in which someone moves from ‘some’ to ‘not all’, and explain why it is not a logical deduction.</li>\n</ol>\n<details><summary>Answers</summary>\n<ol>\n<li>I (‘some politicians are honest’); O, because ‘not all S are P’ is equivalent to ‘some S are not P’; E; A, because it refers to all dogs.</li>\n<li>It is the contradictory, so ‘some exam is not written’ is true. About E we know nothing: contraries can both be false.</li>\n<li>Both colour everything except the shared area (regions 10, 01 and 00). It is the second De Morgan law.</li>\n<li>Yes: it is <strong>Ferio</strong> (EIO, first figure). E empties the shared area of ‘reptile’ and ‘has hair’; the ✕ of I goes in ‘pet and reptile’, which is only left white outside ‘has hair’.</li>\n<li>Open answer. For example: ‘Some of my friends are coming to the party’, said to suggest that not all of them are coming. That is suggested by the conversation, not by logic: I is compatible with A.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>"}});
